          "title": "Valve Function and Prevention of Backflow",
          "description": "The heart's four valves serve a critical function: preventing blood from flowing backward during the cardiac cycle. The two atrioventricular valves\u2014tricuspid and mitral\u2014sit between the atria and ventricles and prevent backflow into the atria when the ventricles contract. The two semilunar valves\u2014pulmonary and aortic\u2014sit between the ventricles and their outflow arteries and prevent backflow into the ventricles when the ventricles relax and pressure in the arteries exceeds pressure in the ventricles. Each valve is shown in two states: open (allowing forward flow) and closed (sealing the opening). During ventricular contraction, the atrioventricular valves are closed and the semilunar valves are open. During ventricular relaxation, the reverse is true. The flap-like leaflets of each valve physically seal the opening when closed, and the pressure gradients across the valve determine whether it opens or closes. You can step through each valve's states to visualize how the leaflets move."
